# MODULE 7: TESTING AND ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

Before considering the implementation complete, test all the following areas.

## A. Salary & Payroll

1. Create a monthly salary structure for a doctor.
2. Create a daily salary structure and calculate salary using approved working days.
3. Create a commission-based salary structure and verify commission calculations.
4. Verify allowances and deductions.
5. Record full and partial salary payments.
6. Verify paid and due amounts.
7. Finalize payroll and confirm historical figures remain unchanged after later salary structure changes.
8. Confirm ordinary doctors cannot access salary information.
9. Verify adjustments require authorization and audit records.

## B. Global Feature Controls

1. Developer can enable and disable individual modules.
2. Main Doctor / Super Admin cannot change developer-only global toggles.
3. Disabling a feature hides its interface and blocks backend functionality.
4. Direct URL and API access are blocked when a feature is disabled.
5. Existing records remain preserved when a feature is disabled.
6. Re-enabling a feature restores authorized access.
7. Newly added features can be registered in the centralized feature management system.

## C. Backup & Export

1. Developer can generate a complete clinic backup.
2. Super Admin / Main Doctor can generate a complete clinic backup.
3. Ordinary doctors can export only their own permitted data.
4. Verify one doctor cannot access another doctor's backup.
5. Test all date presets and custom date ranges.
6. Verify each module uses the correct date field.
7. Verify complete backup without date restrictions.
8. Verify individual CSV exports.
9. Verify ZIP backup package structure and manifest.
10. Verify Bangla and English text in exported CSV files.
11. Verify special characters, commas, quotation marks, and line breaks.
12. Verify CSV formula-injection protection.
13. Verify backup history and audit logs.
14. Test large exports and attachment handling.
15. Verify failed or partial exports are reported accurately.
16. Confirm unauthorized users cannot access backup files through direct links.
17. Verify that backup packages preserve original IDs and relationships.
18. Verify recovery backup integrity where supported.

## D. Future Subscription and Package Management

1. Developer can create and configure subscription packages.
2. Package names can be changed without modifying application code.
3. Developer can assign features to individual packages.
4. Developer can configure optional usage limits.
5. Global feature toggles work independently of package entitlements.
6. Role permissions work independently of package entitlements.
7. A feature excluded from a clinic's package cannot be accessed by that clinic.
8. Package upgrades and downgrades update feature access correctly.
9. Subscription expiry does not delete patient or clinic data.
10. Manual package assignment and subscription changes are recorded in audit history.

## E. Safe Updates and Data Integrity

1. A new feature can be added without rebuilding the application.
2. New modules can be registered in centralized feature management.
3. Database migrations preserve existing production data.
4. Bug fixes do not unnecessarily affect unrelated modules.
5. Existing functionality continues to work after updates.
6. Backups can be generated before major changes.
7. Application versioning and release documentation are maintained.
8. The architecture remains understandable and maintainable for future developers.
9. Existing patient record counts and relationships are verified after significant migrations.
10. Uploaded clinical documents and attachments remain accessible.
11. No existing patient records are silently deleted or overwritten.
12. Recovery procedures are documented and tested where possible.

## F. Overall Application Quality

1. Test existing patient registration and management.
2. Test doctor and role management.
3. Test visits, appointments, and treatment workflows.
4. Test prescriptions and historical prescription access.
5. Test payment calculations and transaction history.
6. Test search, filters, and dashboards.
7. Test mobile, tablet, and desktop layouts.
8. Test role-based access to all relevant modules.
9. Verify existing functionality remains intact.
10. Verify database integrity and prevent accidental data loss.
11. Test performance with realistic data volumes.
12. Test loading, empty, success, and error states.
13. Test common security vulnerabilities.
14. Test network interruptions and failed operations.
15. Verify the application remains usable on supported browsers.

